--TEST--
A name outside `T`'s public set throws where the key is made rather than where it is used, and `protected` is outside it exactly as an undeclared name is — ADR 0126 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Account {
    public string $owner = "ada";
    public int $balance = 10;
    protected string $note = "kept";
}

Account $a = new Account();

// § 2's conversion is the only door into a `property<T>`, which is what lets
// the access below make no check of its own: a computed name is tested against
// the set when this statement runs, and nothing is accessed on this line.
string $name = "bal" . "ance";
property<Account> $ok = $name as property<Account>;
echo $a->$ok as int, "\n";

// The throw is at the conversion, not at a use — this key is never accessed
// through and it still fails.
try {
    string $missing = "bal" . "ances";
    property<Account> $bad = $missing as property<Account>;
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// § 1 ranges over the *public* declared roster, so a `protected` name and an
// undeclared one are one failure and not two: the message lists what the set
// holds rather than why this name is not in it.
try {
    string $note = "no" . "te";
    property<Account> $peek = $note as property<Account>;
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// ADR 0066 § 3's sugar answers `null` in the same place and for the same
// reason: it is § 2's row with the throw replaced, not a second check.
string $again = "bal" . "ances";
?property<Account> $maybe = $again as ?property<Account>;
echo $maybe == null ? "null" : "some", "\n";
--EXPECT--
10
`"balances"` is not one of `Account`'s public declared properties: `$balance`, `$owner`
`"note"` is not one of `Account`'s public declared properties: `$balance`, `$owner`
null
